\n- **Q: d8-d7**: A passive retreat; no clear benefit now.\n\nAmong these, **B: c8-g4** stands out: it develops actively and targets the knight on g5, which is already somewhat exposed. After ...Bg4, White must decide what to do with the knight — potentially retreating or exchanging, possibly doubling pawns or losing tempo.\n\nAnother strong contender is **N: b8-c6**, which develops harmoniously and prepares central counterplay without overcommitting.\n\nHowever, **B: c8-g4** exerts immediate pressure and could lead to tactical simplification or positional gains.\n\nReason:  \nThe move **B: c8-g4** develops a piece with tempo by attacking the advanced white knight on g5, forcing White to respond (either by moving the knight, playing h3, or sacrificing it). This disrupts White’s coordination and may lead to favorable imbalances. It also avoids passivity and increases pressure before White consolidates.\n\nAnswer: B: c8-g4"}
