 a piece, attacks the center, and prepares to recapture on e5 if needed. It also avoids blocking the c-pawn, keeping future flexibility.\n\nReason:  \nN:b8-c6 is a principled developing move that increases pressure on the center, particularly the knight on e5, while maintaining flexibility. It improves piece activity and prepares potential counterplay in the center. Other moves like c7-c5 are also good, but committing the pawn can be met with d4 by White, which we don’t have an immediate answer to. Nc6 avoids premature pawn commitments and keeps options open.\n\nAnswer: N: b8-c6'}
