----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, R: a8-b8, R: a8-c8, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: c6-a5, N: c6-b4, N: c6-b8, N: c6-d4, N: c6-e5, N: c6-e7, Q: d8-c8, Q: d8-b8, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, Q: d8-e7, Q: d8-f6, Q: d8-g5, K: e8-d7, K: e8-e7, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: f8-e7, B: f8-d6, B: f8-c5, B: f8-b4, B: f8-a3, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nSeveral moves are worth considering. The knight on c6 is well-placed and can exert pressure with N: c6-b4 (attacking the d3 square and potentially forking if White isn't careful), or N: c6-e5, jumping into the center. However, more urgent is the possibility of opening lines for Black’s pieces, especially given that White has neglected development on the queenside and center.\n\nThe pawn push P: a7-a5 gains space and prevents White's b2-b4 ideas while keeping options open. Alternatively, P: b7-b5 could be considered to challenge the center, but it weakens the c5 square and may be premature.\n\nAnother idea is activating the queen with Q: d8-b8 or Q: d8-c8, doubling on the c-file or b-file. But this is slow compared to central activity.\n\nMore compelling is developing active play: N: c6-e5 puts immediate pressure on key central squares, eyes f7 (though not immediately threatening due to distance), and supports potential attacks on d3 or f3. It also avoids being traded off easily and increases coordination.\n\nHowever, the most dynamic and aggressive choice is **Q: d8-f6**, which brings the queen to a strong diagonal, eyeing h4 and pressuring the kingside. It threatens tactical ideas involving ...Bf8-c5 or ...Ne5-g4, potentially targeting f2 if White isn’t careful. While slightly early, in many openings such queen sorties are justified when the opponent hasn’t castled or developed.\n\nBut caution: Qd8-f6 might be met with Ng1-e2 or even f2-f3, kicking the queen and gaining tempo. So perhaps too early.\n\nInstead, **N: c6-e5** stands out: it occupies the strongest central outpost, is hard to dislodge, supports future attacks, and doesn't waste time. It improves position without risk.\n\nAlso notable: **P: f7-f5** prepares kingside expansion and possible counterplay, but weakens e6 and the king’s position—risky without castling or proper prep.\n\nBest balance of safety and initiative: **N: c6-e5**\n\nReason:  \nThe knight move to e5 is one of the strongest central outposts, exerts influence over key squares (d3, f3, g4, c6), and increases Black's activity without weakening the position. It supports potential follow-ups like ...Ne5-g4 or ...Nf3+ ideas if White isn’t careful. It’s a principled, high-impact developing move that maximizes pressure in the center.\n\nAnswer: N: c6-e5"}
